      "explanation": "Acidic nature depends upon nature of electron withdrawing group and electronegativity. Electronegativity further depends on % of s character. Higher the s-character, greater will be the electronegativity and hence tendency to loose H increases thus acidic character also increases.<br><br> <img src=\"https://imagex.cdn.examgoal.net/1ksry43m6/ff82f8b9-0d19-4f4d-8db9-6d9db27d6454/74e1ddf0-05e2-11ec-aff4-f93815541f69/file-1ksry43m7.jpeg\" style=\"max-width: 100%;height: auto;display: block;margin: 0 auto;\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"JEE Advanced 2019 Paper 1 Offline Chemistry - Aldehydes, Ketones and Carboxylic Acids Question 68 English Explanation 1\"> <br><br>sp- hybridisation (50% s character) (pK<sub>a</sub> = 1.86)<br><br> <img src=\"https://imagex.cdn.examgoal.net/1ksry4pni/cd8eda11-852e-4090-819d-4497a7d232fc/85e79fe0-05e2-11ec-aff4-f93815541f69/file-1ksry4pnj.jpeg\" style=\"max-width: 100%;height: auto;display: block;margin: 0 auto;\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"JEE Advanced 2019 Paper 1 Offline Chemistry - Aldehydes, Ketones and Carboxylic Acids Question 68 English Explanation 2\"> <br><br>sp<sup>2</sup> - hybridisation (33-33% s character) (pK<sub>a</sub> = 4.3)<br><br> <picture><source media=\"(max-width: 320px)\" srcset=\"https://imagex.cdn.examgoal.net/1ksry5h8v/721841dc-4f61-47be-91d0-001edc849727/9b3851f0-05e2-11ec-aff4-f93815541f69/file-1ksry5h8w-320w.jpeg\"><source media=\"(max-width: 500px)\" srcset=\"https://imagex.cdn.examgoal.net/1ksry5h8v/721841dc-4f61-47be-91d0-001edc849727/9b3851f0-05e2-11ec-aff4-f93815541f69/file-1ksry5h8w-500w.jpeg\"><img src=\"https://imagex.cdn.examgoal.net/1ksry5h8v/721841dc-4f61-47be-91d0-001edc849727/9b3851f0-05e2-11ec-aff4-f93815541f69/file-1ksry5h8w.jpeg\" style=\"max-width: 100%;height: auto;display: block;margin: 0 auto;\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"JEE Advanced 2019 Paper 1 Offline Chemistry - Aldehydes, Ketones and Carboxylic Acids Question 68 English Explanation 3\"></picture> <br><br>sp<sup>2</sup> - hybridisation (Resonance effect) (pK<sub>a</sub> = 4.5)<br><br> <img src=\"https://imagex.cdn.examgoal.net/1ksry5zl4/875e67d7-ce22-4b55-9228-8cb8ba468635/a9635590-05e2-11ec-aff4-f93815541f69/file-1ksry5zl5.jpeg\" style=\"max-width: 100%;height: auto;display: block;margin: 0 auto;\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"JEE Advanced 2019 Paper 1 Offline Chemistry - Aldehydes, Ketones and Carboxylic Acids Question 68 English Explanation 4\"> <br><br>sp<sup>3</sup> - hybridisation (25% s-character) (pK<sub>a</sub> = 4.8)<br><br>Hence, acidic order I &gt; II &gt; III &gt; IV.<br><br>II is more acidic than III since electron donating group (<mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.375ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"8.074ex\" height=\"1.97ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -705 3568.6 870.6\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"2212\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2212\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(778,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D442\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D442\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(1541,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D436\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D436\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(2301,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"msub\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D43B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D43B\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(864,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"33\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-33\"></use></g></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container>) is attached to benzene ring in III which decreases the acidic character.<br><br>On the other hand, pK<sub>a</sub> value also determined acidic nature, lower pK<sub>a</sub> value gives maximum acidic character.<br><br>Hence, option (b) is correct."
